<section>
  <h2>Explanation</h2>
  <p>
    A particle moves on a line with a given velocity
    <i>v</i>(<i>t</i>) = <i>p</i> + <i>q t</i> + <i>r t</i>². Differentiate
    for acceleration and integrate for displacement from rest at the origin.
    <i>r</i> = 0 is constant acceleration; <i>r</i> = 0 and <i>q</i> = 0 is
    constant velocity.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is still closed form — a quadratic <i>v</i>(<i>t</i>) integrates to
    a cubic. There is no numerical stepping.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><i>a</i>(<i>t</i>) = d<i>v</i>/d<i>t</i> = <i>q</i> + 2<i>r t</i></li>
    <li><i>s</i>(<i>t</i>) = ∫<i>v</i> d<i>t</i> = <i>p t</i> + ½<i>q t</i>² + ⅓<i>r t</i>³, taking <i>s</i>(0) = 0</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The live values are those three functions at the current time. Run
    advances <i>t</i> to 4 s so you can watch <i>s</i> grow and, with the
    default coefficients, see the particle come to rest and turn back.
  </p>
</section>
